      "id": "seg:code:discussion:0011:0001",
      "blockId": "code:discussion:0011",
      "type": "preserve",
      "source": "```swift\nstruct Point {\n    let x: Int, y: Int\n}\n\nlet p = Point(x: 21, y: 30)\nprint(String(reflecting: p))\n// Prints \"p: Point = {\n//           x = 21\n//           y = 30\n//         }\"\n```",
      "normalizedSource": "```swift struct Point { let x: Int, y: Int } let p = Point(x: 21, y: 30) print(String(reflecting: p)) // Prints \"p: Point = { // x = 21 // y = 30 // }\" ```",
      "sourceHash": "c1be70aedfc3c2fe81138794dc262c05d96f9a437428c513575fba3bc89838ef",
      "translatable": false,
      "preserveReason": "code",
      "contextBefore": "For example, this custom `Point` struct uses the default representation supplied by the standard library.",
      "contextAfter": "After adding `CustomDebugStringConvertible` conformance by implementing the `debugDescription` property, `Point` provides its own custom debugging representation.",
      "documentTitle": "init(reflecting:)",
      "framework": "Swift",
      "ko": "```swift\nstruct Point {\n    let x: Int, y: Int\n}\n\nlet p = Point(x: 21, y: 30)\nprint(String(reflecting: p))\n// Prints \"p: Point = {\n//           x = 21\n//           y = 30\n//         }\"\n```",
      "status": "preserved",
